WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:04.243
- An avalanche of problems
within the US Postal Service

00:00:04.243 --> 00:00:07.253
could keep votes from
being counted in 2020.

00:00:08.510 --> 00:00:10.141
More Americans than ever before

00:00:10.141 --> 00:00:12.870
are expected to vote by mail this year.

00:00:12.870 --> 00:00:16.200
And since the USPS has a
monopoly over letter delivery,

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:19.360
tho se ballots will have to
go through the postal service.

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:22.700
But years of financial
problems, recent budget cuts,

00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:25.830
and a strain from the pandemic
is slowing the system down

00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:27.313
threatening our democracy.

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:30.920
- In 19 states, your ballot needs

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:33.370
to be postmarked by a
certain date to count.

00:00:33.370 --> 00:00:35.080
Now, only the US Postal Service

00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:37.350
can issue a legally valid postmark.

00:00:37.350 --> 00:00:39.140
FedEx cannot postmark your ballot.

00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:40.450
UPS cannot postmark your ballot.

00:00:40.450 --> 00:00:42.750
Only the Postal Service can do that.

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:45.410
Narrator: The problems
begin with sorting machines,

00:00:45.410 --> 00:00:47.523
which are crucial to a quick delivery.

00:00:47.523 --> 00:00:49.740
But the Postal Service has been planning

00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:52.130
on dismantling them since May.

00:00:52.130 --> 00:00:53.501
And many have already been removed

00:00:53.501 --> 00:00:56.270
from distribution centers
across the country,

00:00:56.270 --> 00:00:58.060
which could slow down all sorts

00:00:58.060 --> 00:01:01.240
of mail delivery, including ballots.

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:04.060
- Ballot and election mail
don't get special treatment

00:01:04.060 --> 00:01:07.020
on their own in the postal
system for being election mail.

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:08.040
They're just processed in with

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:09.713
all the other first-class mail.

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:12.510
Narrator: Mail-in voting includes both

00:01:12.510 --> 00:01:16.230
universal mail-in ballots
and absentee ballots.

00:01:16.230 --> 00:01:19.400
Some states send mailed ballots
to every registered voter,

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:21.480
while others require voters to apply

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:24.040
for an absentee ballot
ahead of the election.

00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:26.585
That process results in a lot of mail,

00:01:26.585 --> 00:01:30.863
and state election offices
depend on the USPS to deliver it.

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:36.020
- Only the USPS can go into mailboxes.

00:01:36.020 --> 00:01:38.709
They sort of have this
monopoly over letters

00:01:38.709 --> 00:01:41.671
and first-class mail, but not packages.

00:01:41.671 --> 00:01:43.710
Narrator: And if the Postal Service can't

00:01:43.710 --> 00:01:47.120
deliver and return applications
and ballots on time,

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:49.360
those votes might not be counted.

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:52.570
- As a voter, you have a lot
of agency over making sure

00:01:52.570 --> 00:01:55.120
your vote gets counted, even
in a crazy pandemic time.

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:56.250
If you are voting by mail,

00:01:56.250 --> 00:01:58.390
you should go and request your ballot now.

00:01:58.390 --> 00:02:00.280
Do not wait until the last minute.

00:02:00.280 --> 00:02:02.320
This is a concept experts refer to as

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:05.530
flattening the curve
of mail ballot requests

00:02:05.530 --> 00:02:07.530
Narrator: Some states
also offer other ways

00:02:07.530 --> 00:02:09.885
to return your ballot, like in drop boxes

00:02:09.885 --> 00:02:13.303
or by hand delivering them
to local election offices.

00:02:14.430 --> 00:02:17.089
The Postal Service was already
dealing with mail delays

00:02:17.089 --> 00:02:19.610
thanks in part to reforms implemented

00:02:19.610 --> 00:02:22.110
by Postmaster General Louis DeJoy.

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:24.640
Those reforms led to cost-cutting measures

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:28.570
like slashing overtime and
limiting late delivery shifts.

00:02:28.570 --> 00:02:31.750
Financial problems at the USPS aren't new,

00:02:31.750 --> 00:02:33.286
but President Donald
Trump's recent attacks

00:02:33.286 --> 00:02:35.200
on universal mail-in voting

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:38.330
brought national attention to the reforms.

00:02:38.330 --> 00:02:40.742
- They're trying it again with this whole

00:02:40.742 --> 00:02:44.453
80 million mail-in ballots
that they're working on.

00:02:45.330 --> 00:02:48.360
Sending them out to people
that didn't ask for them.

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:50.730
And it's not fair and it's not right.

00:02:50.730 --> 00:02:51.830
- The other part of it is,

00:02:51.830 --> 00:02:55.181
the pandemic has been really,
really scary for people,

00:02:55.181 --> 00:02:57.080
and it's undermined a lot of people's

00:02:57.080 --> 00:02:59.360
faith and trust in their government.

00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:01.483
Narrator: DeJoy told senators
that the Postal Service

00:03:01.483 --> 00:03:03.920
will no longer be dismantling machines

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:05.470
prior to the election,

00:03:05.470 --> 00:03:09.150
but those that were already
removed won't be replaced.

00:03:09.150 --> 00:03:11.290
He also said that postal employees will

00:03:11.290 --> 00:03:14.650
be approved for overtime
to process mail-in votes.

00:03:14.650 --> 00:03:16.630
And in a contentious House hearing,

00:03:16.630 --> 00:03:19.703
DeJoy promised that mail-in
voting would be safe.

00:03:20.987 --> 00:03:22.970
- I am not engaged in
sabotaging the election.

00:03:22.970 --> 00:03:26.770
We will do everything within
our power and structure

00:03:26.770 --> 00:03:28.680
to deliver ballots on time.

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:30.940
Narrator: But many
worry this isn't enough,

00:03:30.940 --> 00:03:32.340
especially because of issues

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:35.330
processing ballots during the primaries.

00:03:35.330 --> 00:03:38.320
- Election offices dealt with
a massive, massive influx

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:40.700
of people requesting mail
ballots at the last minute.

00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:42.380
There were people, a lot
of people in some states,

00:03:42.380 --> 00:03:44.810
who didn't get their
ballots at all, or you know,

00:03:44.810 --> 00:03:46.560
got their ballots on
the day of the election

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:48.970
or when it was too late to return them.

00:03:48.970 --> 00:03:51.290
Narrator: In July, the
Postal Service sent a letter

00:03:51.290 --> 00:03:53.605
to 46 states and Washington, DC,

00:03:53.605 --> 00:03:56.000
warning that their registration deadlines

00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:58.150
might not provide enough time

00:03:58.150 --> 00:04:00.320
for the agency to deliver ballots.

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:03.030
And though concerns about
a contentious election

00:04:03.030 --> 00:04:05.780
are at the forefront of
the Postal Service debate,

00:04:05.780 --> 00:04:08.910
other problems will
persist after November.

00:04:08.910 --> 00:04:10.600
- More people are interested and concerned

00:04:10.600 --> 00:04:13.060
about the post office
than maybe ever before

00:04:13.060 --> 00:04:14.300
because of the impending election.

00:04:14.300 --> 00:04:15.830
But, you know, it's important to step back

00:04:15.830 --> 00:04:16.870
and look at the big picture,

00:04:16.870 --> 00:04:18.670
and the financial problems

00:04:18.670 --> 00:04:20.770
with the Postal Service
are not going away.

00:04:20.770 --> 00:04:22.310
These are big existential questions

00:04:22.310 --> 00:04:23.690
about the post office's future,

00:04:23.690 --> 00:04:24.600
and what it will look like,

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:28.683
that are not going to go
away after the election.

